<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oruç Reis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1474–1518</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Aruj Barbarossa, Oruç Barbarossa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oruç Reis, called Barbarossa, was an Ottoman corsair from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> who, with his brother Hayreddin, raided Spanish shipping and coasts from bases in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. He lost an arm attacking Spanish-held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bejaia</a> in 1512, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in 1516 and made himself its ruler. He was killed by Spanish forces near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> in 1518, and Hayreddin then placed Algiers under the Ottoman sultan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oruc-reis.jpg" alt="Oruç Reis" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Theodor de Bry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arolsen_Klebeband_01_465_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aruj, known to the Turks as Oruç Reis, was born on the Ottoman island of Midilli, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a>. His father, Yakup Ağa, was an Ottoman official of Turkish or Albanian descent who took part in the Ottoman conquest of the island from the Genoese in 1462 and received a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> there. He married a local Greek woman named Katerina, and they had four sons, Ishak, Aruj, Hızır and Ilyas, and two daughters. Yakup worked as a potter and traded his wares by boat. The brothers went to sea, and Aruj, Ilyas and Hızır turned privateers against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>.</p><p>During a trading voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, a Hospitaller galley attacked Aruj and Ilyas. Ilyas was killed and Aruj was wounded and held for nearly three years in the Knights' castle at Bodrum. He escaped from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> ship and later joined a captain named Ali.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Ottoman prince Şehzade Korkut gave Aruj galleys at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalya">Antalya</a> and later at İzmir, and after Korkut fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qansuh-al-ghawri">Qansuh al-Ghawri</a> gave him a ship and sent him to raid Christian coasts. In 1503 he made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djerba">Djerba</a> his base, and Hızır joined him. In 1504 the Hafsid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> allowed them the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-goulette">La Goulette</a> on condition of one third of the booty. Between 1504 and 1510 Aruj carried Muslim refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, which earned him the name Baba Aruj, Father Aruj, which in Spain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> became Barbarossa.</p><p>In August 1512, helping the exiled ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bougie</a> against the Spaniards, Aruj lost his left arm and afterwards used a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prosthesis">prosthesis</a>, which gave him the nickname Silver Arm. In 1516 the brothers freed Jijel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> from the Spaniards but took control of them, forcing out the Zayyanid ruler Abu Hammu Musa III. Aruj declared himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Algiers, took Miliana, Medea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenes">Ténès</a>, and in 1518 took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> and executed its ruler Abu Zayan, whom the Spaniards had appointed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Spanish forces and their allies moved against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, and Aruj, betrayed by local people, tried to break out and return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. Hayreddin's diary says he turned back to fight beside his soldiers and died of a spear wound in May 1518; the Spaniards, who beheaded the corpse, took the head to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as proof of his death. The brother Hızır, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa">Hayreddin Barbarossa</a>, inherited his place and name and became the most powerful Ottoman admiral. Aruj is credited with establishing an Ottoman presence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> that lasted four centuries, and a statue of him was inaugurated at Aïn Témouchent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aruj Barbarossa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Spain took Bejaia and Tripoli in 1510, while the brothers <b>Oruç</b> and Hayreddin Barbarossa operated from La Goulette and Jerba with Hafsid permission before founding their own state at Algiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1516 the town’s notables called on the corsair <b>Oruç Barbarossa</b>, a native of Lesbos who had been raiding Christian shipping from Tunisian and Algerian ports, to help them against the Spanish garrison.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The corsair <b>Oruç Barbarossa</b> attacked Bejaia in 1512 and 1514 and lost his arm in the first attempt.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-bougie">Spanish Bougie</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mytilene, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.11,26.55&amp;year=1502" aria-label="Mytilene on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Oruç Reis"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4771 4221h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4771 4221h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.11,26.55&amp;year=1502">What was here in 1502? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
